1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:13,013
I am the oracle, your personal research memory. Everybody in the lab has an oracle. A chat window forgets; I keep what you found, and you can search those memories whenever you like.

2
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:22,069
I store your key findings, each with the project and experiment it came from, and search them across all your projects, over the years.

3
00:00:22,069 --> 00:00:37,540
I also search your lab notebook: your notes for each day, and the prompts you gave Claude Code, which are recorded automatically. Everything lives in your own Obsidian notes, a folder you can open and browse yourself.

4
00:00:37,949 --> 00:00:51,217
When you choose, I prepare a finding for the lab oracle, the lab's shared memory, and you publish it. Protected and restricted findings, such as patient data or unpublished results, stay in your own notes.

5
00:00:51,217 --> 00:01:00,465
I keep what you found, not what the literature says. I remain factual: if I hold nothing on a topic, I say so.

6
00:01:00,849 --> 00:01:20,293
A real run. In March, in a project on liver nodules, the small growths in a scarred liver that can turn into cancer, the member asks me to remember that the gene GPC3 marks early hepatocellular carcinoma, the first stage of liver cancer, and tells it apart from a nodule that has not turned.

7
00:01:20,293 --> 00:01:24,776
I file the finding with its project and experiment.

8
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:33,126
Eight months later, in a liver screening project, the member passes me six genes and asks what they know about early liver cancer.

9
00:01:33,126 --> 00:01:44,529
I recall GPC3, because early hepatocellular carcinoma is early liver cancer. For the other five, I say plainly that I hold nothing of theirs.

10
00:01:44,950 --> 00:01:55,338
To use me, just ask Claude Code: ask the oracle to remember a finding, or what you know about a topic. Give me the finding and where it came from; I ask for anything missing.

11
00:01:55,338 --> 00:02:05,857
The first line of the reply is the verdict: Remembered, Found, Not found or Unsure. You can open every entry in Obsidian.
